        {
          "id": "p1",
          "text": "As spring weather made the journey more survivable, more ordinary people tried to cross. The data shows a seasonal bump, with apprehensions rising from just over 6,500 in February to over 8,200 in March along the southwest border. Even so, the year-to-date numbers remain near multi-decade lows because of current enforcement rules. The system is holding the line, but it's ordinary people who bear the cost. Meanwhile, the elected officials who set the rules everyone else lives under are stuck in a funding dispute over the agencies that enforce them.",
          "source": {
            "agent_id": "The-Everyman",
            "fragments": [
              "why-0",
              "how-0",
              "result-0",
              "result-1"
            ],
            "channel_role": "toned"
          },
          "warnings": [],
          "fit": 0.85
        },
        {
          "id": "p2",
          "text": "The officers carrying out policy on the ground are seeing a documented rise in border apprehensions between two months. Analysts, or people whose job is to watch the numbers, point to the seasonal climate as a key driver. This activity metric reflects a reality on the shared boundary where policy meets real people. While crossings stay historically low due to asylum and deportation rules, the legislative stalemate in Washington leaves agencies without clear funding direction, a political impasse that affects everyone's daily reality.",
          "source": {
            "agent_id": "The-Everyman",
            "fragments": [
              "how-0",
              "why-0",
              "result-0",
              "result-1"
            ],
            "channel_role": "toned"
          },
          "warnings": [],
          "fit": 0.78
        },
        {
          "id": "p3",
          "text": "Conditions that make the journey more survivable in spring have led to an increase in apprehensions. The statistics are a border activity metric watched by security analysts. For those apprehended at the border—individuals facing the consequences of policy—the enforcement policy outcome is clear: low crossing numbers overall. But this security effect is complicated by a government impasse, where politicians are deadlocked over funding for the very agencies tasked with managing the border area.",
          "source": {
